concept:digital-id-act-2024-sec-72 a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "72 Revocation of approval to participate in the Australian"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-72 ;
    values:originalText "Government Digital ID System Digital ID Regulator must revoke approval if Minister gives a direction to do so (1) The Digital ID Regulator must, in writing, revoke an approval given to an entity under section 62 if the Minister gives a direction under subsection 73(1) to do so. Digital ID Regulator may revoke approval (2) The Digital ID Regulator may, in writing, revoke an approval given to an entity under section 62 if: (a) the Digital ID Regulator reasonably believes that the entity has contravened or is contravening this Act; or (b) the Digital ID Regulator reasonably believes that: (i) there has been a cyber security incident involving the entity; and (ii) the cyber security incident is serious; or (c) if the entity is a body corporate—the entity is a Chapter 5 body corporate (within the meaning of the Corporations Act 2001); or (d) if the entity is an individual—the entity is an insolvent under administration; or (e) the Digital ID Regulator is satisfied that it is not appropriate for the entity to participate in the Australian Government Digital ID System; or (f) circumstances specified in the Digital ID Rules apply in relation to the entity.  Chapter 4  Australian Government Digital ID System" ;
    skos:definition "Government Digital ID System Digital ID Regulator must revoke approval if Minister gives a direction to do so (1) The Digital ID Regulator must, in writing, revoke an approval given to an entity under section 62 if the Minister gives a direction under subsection 73(1) to do so. Digital ID Regulator may revoke approval (2) The Digital ID Regulator may, in writing, revoke an approval given to an entity under section 62 if: (a) the Digital ID Regulator reasonably believes that the entity has contravened or is contravening this Act; or (b) the Digital ID Regulator reasonably believes that: (i) there has been a cyber security incident involving the entity; and (ii) the cyber security incident is serious; or (c) if the entity is a body corporate—the entity is a Chapter 5 body corporate (within the meaning of the Corporations Act 2001); or (d) if the entity is an individual—the entity is an insolvent under administration; or (e) the Digital ID Regulator is satisfied that it is not appropriate for the entity to participate in the Australian Government Digital ID System; or (f) circumstances specified in the Digital ID Rules apply in relation to the entity.  Chapter 4  Australian Government Digital ID System"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "d6df521bf5d676927b9870bd3806636fe98f88caed54eb7ccb3ba193b81e6f87" ;
    cof:pageNumber "93"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:digital-id-act-2024-sec-72-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:digital-id-act-2024-sec-72-ss-2 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
